Issue: `scripts/issues/017-tui-dropdown-select-component.md`

**Before:** the Lua menu had checkbox, flag, multistate, action and text items. Choosing one value from a list meant cycling a multistate item blind, and there was no way to offer a list known only at run time.

**After:** two new item types. `select` reads `Server: [GPU Server ▼]` closed and opens on space into a box of options drawn on top of the menu. `select_dynamic` fetches its options each time it opens.

## How

- `libs/menu.lua`:
  - option helpers (normalize, parse `value|label|description|reason` lines, width-aware truncation);
  - `menu.select_open/close/move/jump/choose`, plus left/right cycling on a closed item;
  - `render_dropdown`, drawn after the footer so it sits on top. It opens below the item, or above when there is more room there, shows at most ten rows with a scrollbar in its right border, and keeps the cursor in view;
  - a focus trap at the top of the key loop;
  - the description area shows the option under the cursor and a disabled option's reason, and the footer shows the dropdown's keys;
  - the command preview adds `flag value`.
- `libs/lua-menu.sh`: `menu_set_item_options ID "value|label|desc|reason" ...` and `menu_set_item_options_command ID "cmd"`, sent to Lua as JSON (`_menu_options_json`).
- `libs/menu-runner.lua`: looks for `dkjson` in its own checkout (`scripts/libs/../../libs/lua/`) before the house path, so a second checkout runs its own vendored copy.
- Documentation: the three `README-lua-menu*.md` files.

## Decisions

- **The preview writes `--flag value`**, like multistate items, rather than the issue example's `--flag=value`. Every item that carries a value then uses one form, and the preview's flag colouring already recognises it.
- **Inside the dropdown, only j, k and q are commands.** Every other letter or digit jumps by first letter, so "GPU Server" is reachable by `g`. HOME/END take the place of g/G.
- **The cursor stops at the ends. The letter jump wraps.**
- **A disabled option can sit under the cursor**, so its reason can be read. Choosing it is refused and the box stays open.
- **At most ten rows show**, fewer when the screen has less room.
- **A fixed list with no value starts on its first option that can be chosen.** A dynamic list starts empty, `(none)`, so that no command runs before anyone opens the dropdown.
- **"loading..." shows while a dynamic list is fetched.** The list is kept until the next opening, so neither drawing nor h/l ever fetches. A failed or empty fetch flashes the item red and says why.
- **Step 16 (animation, marked optional) is not done.** The pure-bash fallback `libs/menu.sh`, used only when LuaJIT is missing, does not get the new types.

## Tested

- `libs/test-menu-screen.sh` (`script` plus the screen replay): 29 new checks. They cover:
  - the closed display, `(none)`, and the command preview;
  - opening, the ● mark, all options listed, and the description;
  - choosing with j and space, then escape;
  - a refused disabled option with its reason, the box staying open;
  - letter jumps, including `g`;
  - h/l cycling past the disabled option;
  - a dynamic list fetched by a command;
  - a 15-option list below 15 checkboxes, which opens upward, shows 10 rows, scrolls to n13 with a 6-row thumb in a 10-row bar, and chooses it.
- Result: **36 passed, 0 failed**, including the 7 earlier checks.
- `test-menu-clipboard.sh` fails here exactly as on main: there is no graphical session to copy to.

